      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here are the remaining examples from where the list got cut off, followed by a few more that focus on assumptions about competence, leadership capability, or \"cultural fit\":\n\n9. You're given a \"developmental\" workshop to \"help\" you improve your communication skills, implying that you're not already effective.\n10. A white colleague takes credit for your idea in a meeting, saying \"I was thinking of that too\" without acknowledging your contribution.\n11. You're excluded from a high-profile project because the team is \"too diverse\" and it's \"better\" to have a \"unified vision\".\n12. A manager says you're \"a great asset\" to the company, but only in a role that plays to your perceived cultural strengths, rather than challenging you to grow.\n13. You're asked to \"mediation\" between coworkers who are having a disagreement, implying that you're more empathetic or able to \"connect\" with both parties because of your background.\n14. A white team member assumes you're familiar with a particular industry or market because of your ethnicity, despite having no relevant experience.\n15. You're given a \"cultural ambassador\" role, where you're expected to \"represent\" the company at events",
